uld not learn that.\n  - MNIST weights are also usually strongest in the centre and fade towards the box edges. Here the edges dominate.\n  - The strict zero border also does not match MNIST's centre-of-mass placement, which lets ink occasionally spill outside the 20×20 box.\n- **For Fashion-MNIST.** The features read naturally as clothing silhouettes:\n  - The empty lower-centre strip is the gap between trouser legs (class 1), and sandals also have gaps.\n  - The left and right edges in the middle rows are sleeves on tops and coats.\n  - The top-centre pattern is a neckline.\n  - Unit 5 groups the footwear and bag (sandal, sneaker, bag, ankle boot) against the upper-body garments.\n  - Sandals (class 5) are the sparsest, most distinctive class.\n- **Against natural-image datasets.** Grayscale CIFAR-style images would give smooth, low-frequency weights everywhere, not crisp fixed-position edges.\n- **The 20×20 box.** Native Fashion-MNIST uses the full 28×28 frame. This version therefore looks like Fashion-MNIST shrunk to 20×20 and zero-padded, but the content cues point clearly to clothing items.\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion-MNIST (Zalando article images), apparently rescaled into the central 20×20 region of the 28×28 frame.\n\n**Classes:** 0 T-shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence:** 55%",
